`schema check --schema-set` is the offline half of the RFC 08 §7 decode seam —
"needs no session at all — that is the whole point" (`cmd/schema.rs`). It is
also the clearest place in the tool where the 0/1/2 contract carries three
*different* facts rather than degrees of one.

**0 — it conforms.**

```console
$ zenctl schema check --schema-set tests/cmd/fixtures/sysinfo.schemaset.json --type Health --from '{"status":"ok"}'
{"detail":[],"kind":"json-schema","type":"Health","verdict":"valid"}

```

**1 — it was checked, and it does not conform.** A finding, with the failing
constraint named.

```console
$ zenctl schema check --schema-set tests/cmd/fixtures/sysinfo.schemaset.json --type Health --from '{"status":"melted"}'
? 1
{"detail":["/status: /"melted/" is not one of /"ok/", /"degraded/" or /"down/""],"kind":"json-schema","type":"Health","verdict":"invalid"}

```

**2 — the check never happened.** Reserved so CI can tell "nonconformant" from
"unobservable" — the same split `cutover` and `probe` guard, and the reason
silence is never a verdict (RFC 05 §3.1).

```console
$ zenctl schema check --schema-set tests/cmd/fixtures/sysinfo.schemaset.json --type Nope --from '{}'
? 2
not checked: tests/cmd/fixtures/sysinfo.schemaset.json carries no type "Nope"

```
